    "sample_id": "test:4702",
    "setup_id": "05601a00c6bc4954635863826740b7b61d18798ad2f99e32a9fef415a652990f",
    "text": "To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( -90 \\le n \\le 90 \\) and \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 419^\\circ\\), we need to use the periodic and symmetric properties of the sine function. The sine function is periodic with period \\(360^\\circ\\), and it is also symmetric about \\(180^\\circ\\). This means that \\(\\sin(360^\\circ - x) = -\\sin(x)\\) and \\(\\sin(180^\\circ + x) = -\\sin(x)\\).\n\nFirst, we reduce \\(419^\\circ\\) to an equivalent angle within the range \\(0^\\circ\\) to \\(360^\\circ\\):\n\\[419^\\circ = 360^\\circ + 59^\\circ.\\]\nThus, \\(\\sin 419^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\).\n\nNext, we need to find an angle \\(n\\) within the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\) such that \\(\\sin n^\\circ = \\sin 59^\\circ\\). Using the properties of the sine function, we know that \\(\\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin 121^\\circ\\). However, \\(121^\\circ\\) is not within the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\). Instead, we use the fact that \\(\\sin 59^\\circ = \\sin (180^\\circ - 59^\\circ) = \\sin (-281^\\circ)\\), but \\(-281^\\circ\\) is not within the range either. The correct equivalent angle within the range is:\n\\[59^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 121^\\circ = -121^\\circ + 180^\\circ = 180^\\circ - 121^\\circ = 59^\\circ.\\]\nSince \\(59^\\circ\\) is already within the range \\(-90^\\circ \\le n \\le 90^\\circ\\), we have:\n\\[n = 59.\\]\n\nThus, the integer \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{59}\\).",
